Matlab
------

In Matlab, the window functions are part of the Signal Processing Toolbox.

See:

https://nl.mathworks.com/help/signal/referencelist.html?type=function

The following 19 window functions are supported by Matlab (v2025a, 2025):

- barthannwin
- bartlett
- blackman
- blackmanharris
- bohmanwin
- boxcar (deprecated; use rectwin instead)
- chebwin
- flattopwin
- gausswin
- hamming
- hann
- hanning
- kaiser
- nuttallwin
- parzenwin
- rectwin
- taylorwin
- triang
- tukeywin

Octave
------

In Octave, Most window functions are part of the "signal" package.

See:

    https://gnu-octave.github.io/packages/signal/
    https://octave.sourceforge.io/signal/overview.html#Window_Functions

In Octave, the following 4 window functions are available by default (no need to load the "signal" package):

- bartlett
- blackman
- hamming
- hanning

In Octave, the following 21 window functions are only available after loading the "signal" package that
must be explicitly loaded before any of the window functions can be called:

- barthannwin
- blackmanharris
- blackmannuttall
- bohmanwin
- boxcar
- chebwin
- expwin
- flattopwin
- gaussian
- gausswin
- hann
- kaiser
- nuttallwin
- parzenwin
- poisswin
- rectwin
- taylorwin
- triang
- tukeywin
- ultrwin
- welchwin

Compiling Octave
----------------

(1) Check for the latest version and download its source, e.g.

    wget https://ftp.nluug.nl/pub/gnu/octave/octave-10.2.0.tar.xz

(2) Prior to the "configure" step, remove stale local files:
    rm -rf ~/.local/share/octave

(3) Perform standard configure / make / make install sequence.

    The "config" step takes about 1.5 minutes.
    The "make" step takes about an hour(!)

(4) Start octave, and install the "signal" package using the 'pkg' command.
    Note that the signal package depends on the control package, so that has to be
    installed, too:

    pkg install -forge control signal

